# an ending
@title Hollow
@bg field
@mood calm

narrate He walked away.
narrate That is the ending nobody reaches by accident, and it is the ending of a reader who spent three books saying no.
narrate He came to the field, and looked at a hole in the ground with grass right up to the edge, and thought about it.
corin neutral "No."
cael startled "You have the mark—"
corin resolute "I know. And I'm not using it. And I'm not giving it away, and I'm not asking for her voice, and I'm not asking for the Fever."
corin resolute "It's not mine to do. It never was."
narrate He was wrong, and it cost forty thousand people eleven more years of a rope across a road. Cael did it instead, four months later, alone, without telling anyone.
narrate Corin went back to the Mire and became a farmer.
narrate He never used the mark. He lived to seventy and it was, by every account, a small and ordinary and entirely pleasant life.
narrate He was the only person in three books who touched the deep places and walked away whole. He kept every memory. He remembered the bread — the road, the column, the hand, the half a loaf — for the whole of his life.
narrate He never saved anyone. He never lost anyone either.

@ending hollow
